+---
+node: previews
+---
+
+# Decision: Preview Cache Eviction Policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker's private 12 GiB cache holds rendered contact sheets. The workload mixes two traffic shapes: long sequential screenings that stream thousands of sheets once each, and comparison sessions that repeatedly revisit a small working set across days. The mix varies by production, so no single policy is best for every observed workload, and the wrong policy either flushes the comparison working set during a big screening or keeps abandoned sheets cached for days.
+
+The policy is internal to the preview worker. What must not change under any choice: the fixed `GET /internal/previews/{clip-id}/{revision}` contract (200 JPEG / 202 pending ticket / 404), the four-job FIFO render queue, the 12 GiB byte bound, and the tenet that cache paths and replacement bookkeeping stay private to the preview worker. Nothing in the design promises any particular clip remains cached.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **Gate 1 — No external footprint.** The policy must not add state or protocol visible outside the preview worker: no cross-node pinning, no cache-prewarm API, no change to the pending-response protocol. An option that requires any of these is eliminated. (Inherited from the tenet *keep cache paths and replacement bookkeeping private to the preview worker*.)
+- **Gate 2 — Bounded hard limit.** The policy must admit a completed sheet only after eviction brings cached JPEG data back within 12 GiB, deterministically. Probabilistic or lazy bounds are eliminated.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Scan resilience — major.** A several-thousand-sheet sequential screening must not displace the comparison working set that editors return to between screenings.
+- **Adaptation to project switch — major.** When a production switches, the working set changes within days; the policy must follow the new set without stale favorites retaining capacity for long.
+- **Retention of repeatedly popular clips — moderate.** Sheets touched repeatedly across sessions should out-survive sheets touched once or twice.
+- **Bookkeeping cost — moderate.** Replacement metadata lives on the hot path of a single eight-core worker serving 24 editors; per-request overhead must stay small, and a single worker holds the whole structure in memory.
+- **Tuning burden — minor.** Fewer free parameters means less risk of a miscalibrated pilot configuration, but tuning is a one-time cost paid inside the worker.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+**Description**: A single queue holding cached entries in last-access order. Every request, hit or admission, moves the entry to the most-recently-used position; eviction takes the least-recently-used entry.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Gate 1: **passes** — one queue, private to the worker.
+- Gate 2: **passes** — eviction is deterministic.
+- Scan resilience: **weak** — a sequential screening is the worst case for LRU: each one-use sheet lands in MRU position in turn, so a long screening evicts everything older, including the comparison working set.
+- Adaptation to project switch: **strong** — recency-only; once a clip stops being requested it falls to the tail within one pass of traffic.
+- Retention of repeatedly popular clips: **partial** — popular clips survive between *requests*, but not between *screenings* that touch them.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **strong** — a move-to-front per access is the cheapest structure here.
+- Tuning burden: **strong** — no parameters beyond the byte bound.
+
+**Summary**: cheapest to run, worst exactly on the workload pattern the archive is named for.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+**Description**: Each entry carries an access counter. Hits increment; periodically, all counters are halved (aging) so counts decay and old favorites cannot dominate forever. Eviction takes the lowest-count entry.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Gate 1: **passes** — counters private to the worker.
+- Gate 2: **passes** — deterministic victim selection.
+- Scan resilience: **strong** — one-touch screening sheets never accumulate count; the popular set stays put.
+- Adaptation to project switch: **partial** — aging bounds staleness but yesterday's favorites decay only over multiple aging intervals; a new production's rising set competes against old counts for days.
+- Retention of repeatedly popular clips: **strong** — the criterion is literally what the counter measures.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **weak** — a counter update per hit plus a global aging pass; on a per-hit path already serving 24 editors this is measurable.
+- Tuning burden: **weak** — the aging interval becomes a second parameter interacting with the byte bound.
+
+**Summary**: best protection for the popular set, paid for in hot-path overhead and slow decay of stale favorites.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description**: Three queues. A1in is a FIFO holding cached data for sheets admitted for the first time since their last eviction; on overflow, the oldest entry's data is dropped and its key moves to A1out. A1out is a key-only FIFO recording recently discarded one-use sheets; on overflow, the oldest key is discarded. Am is an LRU queue holding cached data for sheets whose key was found in A1out — i.e., sheets touched a second time — plus everything promoted since. Re-requesting an Am-evicted sheet demotes its key to A1out, so reuse must be re-proven.
+
+**Demonstration**: A 2,000-sheet screening admits each sheet to A1in; A1in overflow cycles them through A1out and discards them. The comparison working set, requested twice within its A1out residence, is promoted to Am and holds its slots through the entire screening. (See the owning design doc's "Private Cache" section for the admission, promotion, and invariant rules, and PREVIEW-006 through PREVIEW-014 for the specified behaviors.)
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Gate 1: **passes** — queues and the A1out key log are private to the worker; the HTTP interface and queue concurrency are unchanged.
+- Gate 2: **passes** — byte-bound admission evicts non-pending victims deterministically (A1in oldest first, then Am LRU).
+- Scan resilience: **strong** — same mechanism as frequency counting: one-use sheets cannot reach Am; two-touch promotion is the gate.
+- Adaptation to project switch: **strong** — recency governs within each queue; once a clip stops being requested it exits Am in one pass, with no counter lag.
+- Retention of repeatedly popular clips: **strong** — Am is exactly the "touched at least twice" set, in LRU order.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **partial** — two extra queues and key-only metadata, but no per-hit counter arithmetic; hits are a move-to-front in Am, misses a FIFO append.
+- Tuning burden: **weak** — three entry capacities within the byte budget must be tuned against real screening workloads; a bad split degrades to LRU-like or over-retains.
+
+**Summary**: buys the scan resilience and retention of frequency counting without counter arithmetic, at the price of two queues and a size split to tune.
+
+## Selection
+
+**2Q is selected.** It is the only option strong on all three workload-behavior criteria (scan resilience, adaptation, retention) — LRU is eliminated by scan resilience, the criterion weighted *major* that the workload hits hardest, and frequency counting pays for the same resilience in hot-path counter arithmetic plus aging-interval tuning while adapting to a project switch more slowly than recency-governed queues. Both alternatives pass the gates; the gates shaped the option set rather than deciding it, which is expected — the decision turns on the workload criteria.
+
+Committing forecloses no path: the queues are private data structures; if the pilot's observed workload contradicts the two-touch assumption, the policy can be replaced without touching the interface, the render queue, or any consumer. Committing obligates: the queue-size split (A1in/A1out/Am entry capacities) is tuned during implementation and monitored against real screening patterns; the pending-job pin and failure-purge invariants in the owning design doc must hold in the implementation; and the deferred single-sheet-over-budget case must not be silently dropped.
+
+The decision is taken *with* the tenet *keep cache paths and replacement bookkeeping private to the preview worker*, not against it: 2Q's extra bookkeeping raises that tenet's stakes — the three queues are worker-private state, which is precisely why the option set was gated on no external footprint.
